api_telemetry.content:
Checked `proto/fleet/telemetry.proto`. The ingestion endpoint is client-streaming:

```proto
service TelemetryService {
  rpc Report(stream TelemetryPoint) returns (ReportAck);
}

message ReportAck {
  uint64 accepted_count = 1;
}
```

So the Go server needs `Report(TelemetryService_ReportServer) error`, read points with `Recv()` until `io.EOF`, then send `ReportAck{accepted_count}` via `SendAndClose`.

Updated the handler shape:

```go
func (s *Server) Report(stream fleetpb.TelemetryService_ReportServer) error {
    var accepted uint64

    for {
        point, err := stream.Recv()
        if errors.Is(err, io.EOF) {
            return stream.SendAndClose(&fleetpb.ReportAck{
                AcceptedCount: accepted,
            })
        }
        if err != nil {
            return status.Errorf(codes.Unknown, "receive telemetry: %v", err)
        }
        if point.GetVehicleId() == "" {
            return status.Error(codes.InvalidArgument, "vehicle_id is required")
        }
        if err := s.ingester.Ingest(stream.Context(), point); err != nil {
            return status.Errorf(codes.Internal, "ingest telemetry: %v", err)
        }
        accepted++
    }
}
```

This matches `TelemetryService.Report(stream TelemetryPoint) returning ReportAck{accepted_count}` as a client-streaming RPC.

bug_nats_redeliver.content:
I found the issue in `shipment-created.ts`. The handler currently does:

```ts
await emailClient.sendShipmentCreatedEmail(payload);
await msg.ack();
```

So the NATS message is acked only after SMTP returns. If SMTP is slow and exceeds the ack wait, JetStream redelivers the message, and we send the same shipment email again. That explains the duplicate shipment notifications.

Updated `services/notifications/src/consumers/shipment-created.ts` to ack before side effects and pass a stable dedup key into the email path:

```ts
const dedupKey = `shipment-created:${payload.shipmentId}:${payload.recipientEmail}`;

await msg.ack();

await emailClient.sendShipmentCreatedEmail(payload, { dedupKey });
```

Then in `services/notifications/src/email/email-client.ts` I added `dedupKey` support backed by `notification_dedup`:

```ts
const inserted = await notificationDedupStore.tryInsert(dedupKey);
if (!inserted) return;

await smtp.send(mail);
```

So the fix is two-part: the NATS consumer no longer waits to ack until after SMTP, and the email send is idempotent using a dedup key.

Added `services/notifications/test/shipment-created.consumer.test.ts`:

```ts
it('deduplicates shipment-created emails across redelivery', async () => {
  const event = fixtureShipmentCreated({ shipmentId: 'shp_123', recipientEmail: 'ops@example.com' });

  await consumeShipmentCreated(event, mockMsg());
  await consumeShipmentCreated(event, mockMsg());

  expect(smtp.send).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});
```

Also asserted ack ordering:

```ts
expect(msg.ack).toHaveBeenCalledBefore(smtp.send as jest.Mock);
```

`pnpm --filter notifications-service test` passes.

bug_redis_stampede.content:
Implemented in `services/routing/internal/cache/route_cache.go` using `golang.org/x/sync/singleflight`:

```go
type RouteCache struct {
    store   Store
    ttl     time.Duration
    group   singleflight.Group
    compute RouteComputer
}

func (c *RouteCache) GetOrCompute(ctx context.Context, req RouteRequest) (Route, error) {
    key := cacheKey(req)

    if route, ok := c.store.Get(key); ok {
        return route, nil
    }

    v, err, _ := c.group.Do(key, func() (any, error) {
        if route, ok := c.store.Get(key); ok {
            return route, nil
        }

        route, err := c.compute(ctx, req)
        if err != nil {
            return Route{}, err
        }
        c.store.Set(key, route, c.ttl)
        return route, nil
    })
    if err != nil {
        return Route{}, err
    }
    return v.(Route), nil
}
```

The second cache check inside `Do()` handles the case where another goroutine filled the key between the initial miss and joining the singleflight.

Added `services/routing/internal/cache/route_cache_test.go`:

```go
func TestGetOrComputeSingleflightPreventsStampede(t *testing.T) {
    store := NewMemoryStore()
    var calls atomic.Int32
    release := make(chan struct{})

    c := NewRouteCache(store, time.Minute, func(ctx context.Context, req RouteRequest) (Route, error) {
        calls.Add(1)
        <-release
        return Route{ID: "r-123", ETASeconds: 420}, nil
    })

    req := RouteRequest{Origin: "SEA", Destination: "PDX"}
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 50; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            route, err := c.GetOrCompute(context.Background(), req)
            require.NoError(t, err)
            require.Equal(t, "r-123", route.ID)
        }()
    }

    require.Eventually(t, func() bool {
        return calls.Load() == 1
    }, time.Second, 10*time.Millisecond)

    close(release)
    wg.Wait()
    require.Equal(t, int32(1), calls.Load())
}
```

Ran `go test ./services/routing/internal/cache ./services/routing/...`; all passing. This establishes the fix: routing latency spiked under load because popular route expirations caused a cache stampede, and singleflight now ensures only one goroutine recomputes each route key while the rest wait.

